<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/knowledge-base/">Knowledge base</a></li><li aria-current="page">MIA Complaint or Appraisal? How Maryland Policyholders Can Pick the Right Path</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · September 17, 2026</p><h1>MIA Complaint or Appraisal? How Maryland Policyholders Can Pick the Right Path</h1><p class="lede">A Maryland Insurance Administration complaint and an appraisal demand solve different problems. Here’s how to tell which one your claim needs.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><p>When a property claim stalls, Maryland policyholders usually hear about two options: filing a complaint with the Maryland Insurance Administration or invoking the appraisal clause. Deciding between an MIA complaint vs appraisal is easier once you see that they answer different questions. One looks at how your insurer behaved. The other decides what your damage is worth.</p>

<h2>What the Maryland Insurance Administration does</h2>
<p>The MIA is the state agency that licenses and regulates insurers doing business in Maryland. Its consumer services staff accept complaints from policyholders and ask insurers to respond in writing. Maryland’s claims-handling standards appear in COMAR Title 31 and in the unfair claim settlement practices provisions of the Insurance Article, which broadly address things like failing to acknowledge communications, failing to investigate promptly, and refusing to pay without a reasonable basis.</p>
<p>An MIA complaint is well suited to problems such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>An adjuster who hasn’t returned calls or emails for weeks.</li>
<li>A denial letter that doesn’t explain which policy provision applies.</li>
<li>Payments that never arrive after an agreed settlement.</li>
<li>Requests for the same documents again and again with no decision.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complaint process is free, and an inquiry from the regulator often gets a stalled file moving. What it generally isn’t built for is choosing between two competing repair estimates. The MIA reviews conduct and compliance; it is not a panel of estimators deciding whether your roof needs 28 squares or 34.</p>

<h2>What appraisal does</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a contractual process inside your policy. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by two of the three sets the amount of loss. It is designed for one task: valuing a covered loss when you and the insurer disagree on the number. It works best when the insurer has accepted coverage and the dispute is about scope, quantities, pricing, matching or overhead and profit. The full sequence is laid out on <a href="/the-appraisal-process/">the appraisal process</a> page.</p>
<p>Appraisal costs money – your appraiser’s fee and half the umpire’s – and it won’t address complaints about delay or unfair treatment. But when the gap is real and well documented, it is often the most direct way to a final number.</p>

<h2>A side-by-side comparison</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>MIA complaint</th><th>Appraisal</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Main focus</td><td>How the insurer handled the claim</td><td>Dollar amount of a covered loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who decides</td><td>Regulator reviews insurer’s response</td><td>Two appraisers and an umpire</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost to you</td><td>No filing fee</td><td>Your appraiser plus half the umpire</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical outcome</td><td>Insurer explanation, corrective action or file movement</td><td>Signed award setting the amount</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decides coverage?</td><td>Can review whether a denial followed the rules</td><td>Generally no</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2>Can you do both?</h2>
<p>Often, yes. The two tracks don’t cancel each other out. A homeowner in Bowie whose adjuster has gone silent might file an MIA complaint to restart communication, then invoke appraisal once the insurer issues a final estimate they disagree with. A condominium board in Ocean City might use appraisal to settle the value of storm repairs while separately raising handling concerns with the regulator.</p>
<p>The key is to keep each track focused. Use the complaint to describe conduct – dates, unanswered requests, unexplained decisions. Use appraisal to argue numbers with estimates, photos and measurements.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Good-faith claims are a legal step</b>Maryland law allows certain first-party policyholders to pursue an insurer’s failure to act in good faith, and that process generally begins with a complaint filed with the MIA before any court action. It has its own requirements and timing. If you are considering it, speak with a Maryland attorney first – appraisers don’t handle these claims.</div>

<h2>Choosing your starting point</h2>
<h3>Start with the MIA when…</h3>
<p>The insurer isn’t responding, hasn’t explained a decision, or seems to be ignoring the documents you’ve sent. Until there is a clear, final position from the carrier, there may be nothing concrete to appraise.</p>
<h3>Start with appraisal when…</h3>
<p>The insurer has accepted the loss, issued an estimate, and stands behind a number you think is too low. If you can point to specific line items – missing roof slopes, unpriced code work, plaster replaced with drywall at a lower cost – appraisal speaks directly to those gaps.</p>
<h3>Start with an attorney when…</h3>
<p>The claim has been denied on coverage grounds, the insurer is alleging misrepresentation, you have been asked to sit for an examination under oath, or a deadline to sue may be approaching. Those issues carry legal consequences that neither the MIA complaint process nor appraisal is meant to manage alone.</p>

<h2>Getting the estimates right first</h2>
<p>Whichever path you choose, clarity about the numbers helps. A line-by-line <a href="/property-claim-services/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> shows where the insurer’s estimate falls short and by roughly how much. That gives you specifics for an MIA complaint if the shortfall reflects a handling problem, and a roadmap for appraisal if it reflects a valuation disagreement.</p>
<p>Maryland Insurance Appraiser doesn’t file regulatory complaints or give legal advice, but we can tell you whether a dispute looks like an appraisal case and what the evidence supports. For more on fees, timing and the regulator, browse our <a href="/common-questions/">common questions</a>.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/knowledge-base/baltimore-rowhouse-roof-claims/"><small>Guide</small><span>Baltimore Rowhouse Roof Claims: Flat Roofs, Party Walls and Getting the Scope Right</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/knowledge-base/bay-hurricane-deductibles/"><small>Guide</small><span>Hurricane and Named-Storm Deductibles Around the Chesapeake Bay: What Maryland Owners Should Check</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/knowledge-base/western-maryland-ice-dam-claims/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Dam Claims in Western Maryland: Garrett and Allegany County Winter Damage Explained</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a straight answer on whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="box-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19